  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThe main story today is the wind. Southerly winds are expected \nto gust 35-45 MPH through the late morning and afternoon hours. \nThe northwestern half of the area has the highest chance to see \npeak winds exceed 45 MPH (30 to 60% per the latest NBM). Despite\nincreasing high clouds, the wind should allow us to once again \nreach the upper 70s and even low 80s in some areas. \n\nA cold front moves into the area for Saturday, resulting in a \nwide range in temperatures. Southeastern areas could once again \napproach 80, but northern areas could struggle to reach 60. \nLight rain showers are possible already in the morning, with \nincreasing coverage in the afternoon. A few rumbles of thunder \ncannot be ruled out, but instability is far too meager to \nsupport any kind of severe threat. Areas most likely to see rain\non Saturday and Saturday night are northwest of a line from \nPhillipsburg to Grand Island to Fullerton. \n\nSunday should start off dry for most of the area before \nadditional rain moves in from the west. Exact timing remains \nuncertain, but many areas, especially east of Highway 281 will \nlikely remain dry until more widespread rain arrives Sunday \nnight and lasts through Monday. Like Saturday, a few rumbles of \nthunder are possible, but severe weather is unlikely. In total, \nareas west of Highway 281 are most favored (30 to 70%) to see \n1.00\" or more of rainfall. \n\nMost of the area should dry out as the system departs on\nTuesday, but another upper trough may bring another brief shot\nof rain to the area Tuesday night through Wednesday morning.\nTemperatures next week will be near or still slightly above our\nclimatological normal for late October (highs in the upper 50s\nand 60s and lows in the upper 30s and 40s).",
